  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nRising pressure heights over surface high pressure \nwill allow area temperature to return closer to normal today and \never so slightly above normal Friday while providing dry weather and \nabundant sunshine.\n\nThis pattern changes as an upper trough drops out of the northern \nplains southeastward towards the Ohio River Valley by Saturday \nmorning before crossing the region by early next week. Lift and some \nmoisture advection ahead of the trough axis is likely to result in \nincreased shower and thunderstorm chances. Little more can be said \nat this point due to large variances in the shape and eastward \nprogression of this trough creating difference precipitation \naccumulation (along with environmental threats).  \n\nThe range of outcomes seems to be bounded by 1) a more progressive \ntrough hinted by some of the ensembles results in lower \naccumulation; and 2) a slower and slightly deeper trough favored by \na healthy portion of GEPS and ENS ensemble members in a greater \nprobability for widespread rain accumulations greater than 0.5 \ninches. In general, the timing of the rainfall ranges from late \nSaturday into early Sunday. Additional uncertainty arises in the \ntiming of trough exiting, ranging from Sunday afternoon/evening to \nTuesday. So, long-range PoP outlooks may experience fluctuations in \nthe coming days.\n\nRegardless of the scenarios, the overall hazard risk appears low as \nbuoyancy is likely to be limited (outside of areas of eastern Ohio \nand northwestern West Virginia on Sunday with greater than 80% \nprobability of SBCAPE being less than 1000 J/kg). The only hazard to \nmonitor is flood risks as there could be enough of a Gulf moisture \ndraw to push PWAT values near/above the 90th percentile; if this \noccurs while storm motion is slow and enough instability manifests \nto aid rainfall rates, localized flash flooding risks could \nmaterialize.",
